is. Use it from a plugin or\n`mu-plugin`. First choice, every time."],[716,"There is no `wc_ajax_nopriv_*` hook. If you register only `wc_ajax_<action>`,\nguests reach it too — so your nonce and capability checks are the only gate."],[152,"These are two different questions and custom code needs both:"],[15,"These are two separate implementations of the same feature. Supporting\nboth classic and Blocks checkout means shipping both code paths — there is\nno filter that works on both. Check which one the store actually runs\nbefore building either."],[766,"These getter filters fire constantly — in loops, in the\nadmin, during totals calculation, in REST responses. Keep the callback\ncheap, guard against empty values, and never call the same getter inside the\nfilter (infinite recursion). For cart-line pricing, prefer setting the price\nonce on woocommerce\\_before\\_calculate\\_totals — see\nDeveloper Mental Model."],[1236,"These WordPress action names (`plugins_loaded`, `setup_theme`,\n`after_setup_theme`, `init`, `wp_loaded`, `parse_request`, `wp`,\n`template_redirect`) are long-standing core API and fire in this order on a\nnormal front-end request. WooCommerce's own `init()` method is hooked to\nWordPress `init` at priority `0`; the exact internal sequence is in\nWooCommerce Architecture."],[1304,"Think in the lifecycle, not the page"],[99,"This is Step 1"],[298,"This is the **living architecture** of WooDoc. It is designed for\nsearchability and discoverability: a developer should be able to predict which\nsection a topic lives in."],[1350,"This is the pattern WooDoc is built around. When you see any WooCommerce\nelement — a button, a field, a line of totals, a message, an email — you\nshould be able to walk a short, repeatable path from &#x2A;\"what is this?\"&#x2A; to\n&#x2A;\"where do I safely change it?\"*."],[529,"This is the same pattern HPOS applies to the entire order record: replace\nscattered meta rows with real columns. The product lookup table did it for\none entity years earlier."],[1234,"This page is a WooCommerce-oriented refresher, not a full WordPress course.\nEach section says what the mechanism is, then **how WooCommerce leans on it**."],[1099,"This page is the architectural overview. Individual subsystems (cart totals,\ncheckout, orders, payments) get their own Architecture\npages in later phases."],[382,"This page is the reference definition of WooDoc. Later steps of the project\nshould be measured against it."],[308,"This structure will change as WooDoc grows. Simplified diagrams get replaced\nwith accurate architecture diagrams; sections split or merge as content\narrives. Do not treat it as final."],[1303,"Three layers of \"where a change belongs\""],[556,"Three storage modes"],[787,"To change what a core action *does*, remove its callback and add your own. You\nmust match the **exact** callback identifier and priority used in core."],[1638,"To register a custom status, hook **all three**:\n`register_post_status()` for the status itself,\n`woocommerce_register_shop_order_post_statuses` so it works under HPOS, and\n`wc_order_statuses` to add it to the admin dropdown. Prefix the slug `wc-`."],[369,"Today, WooDoc ships **Introduction**, **Foundations**, **Development**,\n**Commerce Lifecycle**, **Data & Storage**, **Customization**, **Debugging**,\n**Performance**, **Security**, and this **About WooDoc** section. A\ndedicated **Customers** page and **Limitations** in the tree above are\nplanned. The Roadmap sequences the build."],[1698,"Tokens live in `wp_woocommerce_payment_tokens` + `wp_woocommerce_payment_tokenmeta`.\nThe processor holds the sensitive data; WooCommerce stores only its token —\nwhich keeps most stores out of PCI-DSS scope for card data."],[264,"Top-of-page classification strip. Put it directly under the frontmatter."],[462,"Trace every topic to the layer that owns it."],[63,"Tracing which hooks actually fire (and in what order) on a given request is\ncovered in depth on\nHooks — Debugging which hooks fire:\nthe `all` hook trick, reading `wc-template-hooks.php`, and the Query Monitor\nplugin, which lists every hook fired on a request with its registered\ncallbacks and file locations. Reach for that page once logging and the\nstatus report have ruled out an environment problem."],[76,"Transactional emails not arriving"],[1564,"Transitions into `on-hold`"],[1555,"Transitions into `processing`"],[1546,"Transitions into `processing` / `completed` (`pending_to_processing`, `failed_to_processing`, `cancelled_to_processing`, `on-hold_to_processing`, `pending_to_completed`, …)"],[469,"Treat the information architecture as frozen."],[1543,"Triggered by"],[186,"Troubleshooting page"],[1279,"Two behaviour changes to know if you schedule your own jobs: unique-action\nchecks now include the action's **arguments** (previously hook + group only),\nso more actions can be created than before; and failed actions are now\nauto-purged after \\~3 months (`action_scheduler_retention_period_for_failed`,\ndisable via `action_scheduler_enable_failed_action_cleanup`)."],[135,"Two mechanisms exist specifically because a naive WordPress query was too\nslow at scale, and both still have a residual cost to manage:"],[412,"Two principles carry through every page:"],[113,"Two principles run through every page:"],[682,"Two transports"],[1716,"Type"],[410,"Typical documentation hands developers a snippet. That teaches *what to type*,\nnot *why it works*, *where else it could be done*, or *what it puts at risk*.\nWooDoc connects the pieces."],[1615,"Typical trigger"],[610,"Unchanged by HPOS — the same tables serve both legacy and HPOS orders."],[885,"Under the **Checkout Block**, `update_checkout` / `updated_checkout` /\n`payment_method_selected` and the rest of this bus **do not fire**. React\ncomponents subscribe to the `wc/store/checkout` and `wc/store/payment` data\nstores instead."],[1508,"Under the Block, the equivalent server hook is\n`woocommerce_store_api_checkout_update_order_from_request`, plus the\n`ExtendSchema` update callback for data the Block collected."],[393,"Understand how WooCommerce interacts with WordPress."],[394,"Understand what WooCommerce can and cannot realistically do."],[388,"Understand where functionality comes from."],[390,"Understand WooCommerce's internal architecture."],[1580,"Use"],[1756,"Use"],[271,"Use `Callout` for short asides. Use `SafetyLevel` whenever the aside is\nspecifically about *where a customization belongs* — that keeps the\nsafety-level colors meaningful."],[1084,"Use block settings, then Slot/Fill, then the integration interface. Do not\nreach for PHP template overrides."],[1104,"Use it for"],[1081,"Use it. Done. Highest safety, zero maintenance."],[715,"Use the `wc-ajax` path instead when the call is high-frequency and does not\nneed the admin environment. It fires **one** hook for logged-in and guest\nusers alike:"],[1082,"Use the action at that position."],[306,"Useful for advanced or niche scenarios."],[542,"User meta"],[1500,"Validation"],[1518,"Validation"],[756,"Value vs. display — the recurring trap"],[888,"Values passed from PHP (via an `IntegrationInterface`) are read with\n`getSetting()` from `@woocommerce/settings`."],[1708,"Variable products and variations"],[989,"Variables in scope"],[153,"Verify a nonce — `check_ajax_referer()` for AJAX,\n`wp_verify_nonce()` elsewhere. A nonce proves the request came from your\nown page, not that the requester is *allowed* to do this."],[98,"Version context"],[419,"Version-specific and classic-vs-Blocks differences are called out\nexplicitly."],[162,"View/edit orders in bulk, or one specific order"],[1341,"Walking each question"],[1273,"Want to *add markup* next to the price? An **action** at that position."],[1274,"Want to *change the price value*? A **filter** on the value."],[407,"Wants architecture, extension points, internal data flow, debugging strategy, performance, safe patterns, and an honest account of limits"],[894,"wc/v3 REST API"],[1309,"We introduce the idea here and link outward. Later steps of WooDoc expand\neach layer in depth."],[1675,"Webhooks / IPN"],[429,"What can break?"],[559,"What does not change"],[428,"What happens internally?"],[430,"What is the recommended approach?"],[422,"What is this?"],[1424,"What it does **not** do out of the box includes subscriptions, bookings,\nmemberships, multi-vendor marketplaces, advanced product configurators, deep\nERP/accounting integration, and many region-specific tax or invoicing\nrequirements. Those are extensions or custom development."],[1718,"What it is"],[507,"What lives in WordPress's tables"],[95,"What problem it solves"],[1366,"What problem WooCommerce solves, why WordPress alone is not an e-commerce platform, what WooCommerce adds, the trade-offs of choosing it, and when it is the wrong tool."],[427,"What should I **not** change?"],[448,"What Step 1 delivered"],[1460,"What the cart is"],[398,"What they struggle with"],[1305,"What this means in practice"],[1475,"What was added"],[1369,"What WooCommerce adds"],[223,"What WooCommerce does behind the scenes."],[1370,"What WooCommerce provides out of the box"],[508,"What WooCommerce's own tables hold"],[93,"What WooDoc is"],[1393,"What you get"],[96,"What you will learn"],[217,"When a developer should use it."],[1639,"When an order's status changes, WooCommerce fires a cascade — hook the most\nspecific one that fits:"],[991,"When not to override"],[425,"When should I use it?"],[1326,"When something behaves unexpectedly, the first diagnostic question is usually\n*which stage of this lifecycle am I in, and what code runs at that stage* —\nnot *which template rendered this*."],[1270,"When the Performance section &#x2A;(planned)* is written, autoloaded option\nweight from WooCommerce and its extensions gets its own treatment —\nit is one of the highest-leverage things to audit on a slow store."],[511,"When to query directly vs. use the CRUD layer"],[1375,"When WooCommerce is a good choice"],[1376,"When WooCommerce may not be a good choice"],[207,"Whenever a topic is technical, structure it around these sections — use them\nintelligently, not mechanically. Not every page needs every one."],[531,"Where"],[431,"Where do I go next?"],[423,"Where does it live?"],[1338,"Where Does This Come From?"],[1033,"Where each mechanism's code belongs"],[1779,"Where tax display is decided"],[1499,"Where the field list comes from"],[100,"Where to go next"],[69,"Where to look"],[1374,"Where WooCommerce fits compared with a custom application"],[123,"Where WooCommerce spends time and how to reduce it — object caching and sessions, autoloaded options, query and fragment cost, extension overhead, and headless considerations."],[1751,"Whether a sale is \"on\" also depends on the sale schedule\n(`_sale_price_dates_from` / `_sale_price_dates_to`). `wc_scheduled_sales()`\nruns on the **daily** `woocommerce_scheduled_sales` cron event to start and\nend scheduled sales — so a sale ending mid-day is not cleared to the minute."],[1792,"Whether cart totals show inc/exc tax"],[1790,"Whether catalog prices show inc/exc tax"],[1070,"Which gateways are offered at checkout"],[269,"Which layers own a thing."],[898,"Which to use"],[70,"White screen after activating a plugin/update"],[94,"Who it is for"],[1371,"Why businesses choose WooCommerce"],[1372,"Why developers choose WooCommerce"],[424,"Why does it exist?"],[554,"Why HPOS exists"],[724,"Why the extension model is different"],[509,"Why the lookup tables exist"],[1348,"Why this pattern matters"],[213,"Why WooCommerce has this mechanism."],[1365,"Why WooCommerce?"],[1368,"Why WordPress alone is not an e-commerce platform"],[1418,"Without any extensions, a default WooCommerce install can:"],[1754,"WooCommerce 11.0 enables **product object caching by default for new\nstores** and includes query optimizations for large catalogs and variable\nproducts. Reads of `wc_get_product()` may be served from the object cache;\nwrites invalidate it."],[1597,"WooCommerce 11.0 ships an **experimental block-based email editor**, enabled\nunder **WooCommerce → Settings → Advanced → Features** (\"Block Email Editor\n(alpha)\"). Once on, each email's row in **Settings → Emails** gets an\n*Edit* action that opens a block editor for the body instead of the classic\ntemplate. Treat it as unstable and keep template overrides as the supported\npath for now."],[1260,"WooCommerce also registers taxonomies attached to `product`: `product_cat`\nand `product_tag` (the visible catalog taxonomies), `product_type` and\n`product_visibility` (internal, `public => false`, `hierarchical => false`),\nand one taxonomy per global attribute, named `pa_<attribute>` (e.g.\n`pa_color`)."],[134,"WooCommerce and its extensions are frequent contributors: large serialized\nsettings arrays, cached API responses stored as options instead of\ntransients, and (particularly) session and rate-limit rows that should never\nhave been autoloaded in the first place."],[1090,"WooCommerce Architecture"],[471,"WooCommerce architecture: bootstrap, the `WC()` singleton, data stores and\nCRUD classes, sessions."],[1278,"WooCommerce bundles **Action Scheduler**, a more robust queue built on top of\nWordPress. Since Action Scheduler 3.0 it stores jobs in its own tables —\n`wp_actionscheduler_actions`, `wp_actionscheduler_logs`,\n`wp_actionscheduler_groups`, `wp_actionscheduler_claims` — processes them in\nbatches on the `action_scheduler_run_queue` hook (dispatched roughly once a\nminute via WP-Cron), and retries failures. WooCommerce and many extensions\nuse it for deferred emails, webhook delivery, analytics/report generation,\nand data migrations including the HPOS backfill."],[101,"WooCommerce can feel like a black box. A button appears on a page and you\ncannot tell whether it came from a PHP template, a hook, a block, a shortcode,\na script, or an AJAX response. You want to change one number and you are not\nsure what else moves when you do."],[115,"WooCommerce cannot do everything well. WooDoc explains what it does out of\nthe box, what needs customization, what needs an extension, what needs\nexternal infrastructure, and when a different stack (custom PHP, Laravel,\na Node service, a dedicated platform) is the better engineering choice."],[169,"WooCommerce core does not store raw card numbers, and a PCI-compliant\ngateway integration should never let them reach your server either — hosted\nfields or an iframe (Stripe Elements, PayPal, and similar) submit the card\ndirectly to the processor, and your server only ever handles a **token**."],[1170,"WooCommerce Data Model"],[472,"WooCommerce data model overview."],[900,"WooCommerce exposes **two** HTTP APIs built on the WordPress REST\ninfrastructure (`/wp-json/`). They look similar and are used for opposite\npurposes."],[1023,"WooCommerce Extension Model"],[7,"WooCommerce Extension Model is the map\nof the four mechanisms — actions, filters, template overrides, Blocks. This\npage is the applied companion: where the *code* that uses those mechanisms\nshould live, how a Blocks customization actually plays out end to end, and a\nworked library of real requests mapped to a mechanism and a safety level."],[58,"WooCommerce failures rarely have one cause. A methodical order — check the\nlog, check the environment, isolate the conflict, then read the relevant\npipeline — finds the answer faster than guessing at a hook."],[1101,"WooCommerce fires its own actions during this sequence so extensions can hook\nthe right moment:"],[409,"WooCommerce functionality is **distributed**. A single visible element may be\ngoverned by WordPress, WooCommerce core, a template, a hook, a filter, PHP,\nJavaScript, AJAX, a REST endpoint, a block, a database value, an extension, or\na theme customization."],[110,"WooCommerce functionality is distributed. The same visible element can be\ninfluenced by WordPress, WooCommerce core, a template, a hook, a filter, PHP,\nJavaScript, AJAX, a REST endpoint, a block, a database value, an extension, or\na theme customization."],[805,"WooCommerce instantiates `WC_Deprecated_Action_Hooks` and\n`WC_Deprecated_Filter_Hooks` (both extend `WC_Deprecated_Hooks`) during\n`WooCommerce::init()`. They map a set of renamed hooks to their replacements\nand emit a `_deprecated_hook()` notice when the old name is used. Do not rely\non this shim — treat a deprecation notice as \"fix this now\"."],[1098,"WooCommerce is a large plugin with a deliberate internal shape. Once you can\nname its main runtime objects and how they are wired together, reading core\nsource — and predicting where your own code should hook — gets much easier."],[1233,"WooCommerce is a WordPress plugin. Every capability it has is either a\nWordPress mechanism it reuses or a mechanism it adds *in the shape WordPress\nexpects*. If you understand these seven WordPress subsystems, most of\nWooCommerce's structure stops being surprising."],[1035,"WooCommerce is built to be changed without editing it. There are four\nmechanisms for that, and they are not alternatives to pick from at random —\neach operates at a different layer, and real customizations often use two or\nthree together. This page is the map of the system; the Development section\n&#x2A;(planned)* documents each mechanism in depth."],[1100,"WooCommerce loads like any plugin — its main file runs during the \"active\nplugins load\" step of the WordPress request lifecycle.\nFrom there it follows a defined sequence:"],[976,"WooCommerce notices (for full-site-editing themes)"],[536,"WooCommerce order meta"],[712,"WooCommerce passes each nonce in the request's `security` field and checks it\nserver-side with `check_ajax_referer( '<action>', 'security' )`. Under the\nCheckout Block none of these apply — the Store API uses its own\n`Nonce` / `Cart-Token` headers."],[1238,"WooCommerce registers several custom post types:"],[1076,"WooCommerce renders classic storefront fragments from PHP files in its\n`plugins/woocommerce/templates/` directory. Core loads them with\n`wc_get_template()` / `wc_get_template_part()`, which call `wc_locate_template()`\nto resolve the path — checking `yourtheme/woocommerce/<same-path>` first, then\nfalling back to the plugin. Copy a template into that theme folder (minus the\n`templates/` segment) and your copy wins. The resolution can also be filtered\nvia `woocommerce_locate_template` or `wc_get_template`."],[1286,"WooCommerce REST API"],[1539,"WooCommerce sends transactional email itself — one class per email type,\ntriggered by order status transitions\nand a few account events. This page is the structure and the hooks; it does\nnot cover deliverability (SMTP, SPF/DKIM), which is a hosting concern."],[64,"WooCommerce sits between WordPress core, the theme, and every other plugin —\nmost \"WooCommerce is doing something weird\" reports are actually a\nthird-party callback on a WooCommerce hook."],[958,"WooCommerce stores which page holds which shortcode in options\n(`woocommerce_cart_page_id`, `woocommerce_checkout_page_id`,\n`woocommerce_myaccount_page_id`). Those page IDs — not the shortcode text —\nare how core builds cart/checkout URLs and redirects."],[688,"WooCommerce uses AJAX heavily on the classic front-end: adding to cart from a\nloop, updating quantities, applying coupons, refreshing the checkout totals.\nThere are **two** transports, and knowing which one an action uses tells you\nwhere to hook it."],[516,"WooCommerce uses it for"],[1269,"WooCommerce uses options for essentially all store configuration — pages,\ncurrency, tax behavior, checkout options, email settings, gateway settings —\nmostly under a `woocommerce_` key prefix (for example `woocommerce_currency`,\n`woocommerce_cart_page_id`, `woocommerce_calc_taxes`,\n`woocommerce_default_country`). Many are autoloaded; the store's own settings\nplus each active extension's settings all add to the per-request autoload\npayload."],[993,"WooCommerce's **classic** storefront is rendered from PHP template files. A\ntheme can replace any of them by providing its own copy. This is powerful and\nfrequently the *wrong* first choice — a hook or filter usually reaches what\nyou need without the maintenance cost."],[1181,"WooCommerce's data model is a commerce domain expressed in WordPress's\nstorage. Some entities are custom post types; some are custom tables; order\nstorage is currently mid-migration between the two. This page is the map."],[810,"WooCommerce's front-end JavaScript — the core script handles, the jQuery events fired on document.body for cart and checkout, the cart fragments system and its cost, and how the Blocks change the picture."],[130,"WooCommerce's performance story is mostly about **avoiding unindexed lookups\nand unnecessary per-request work** — several of the mechanisms that exist\nfor correctness (sessions, lookup tables, cart fragments) also happen to be\nthe biggest cost centers if left unmanaged. This page connects those dots\nrather than re-deriving them; each links to where the mechanism is\ndocumented in full."],[513,"WooCommerce's storage is two tiers stacked on each other: **WordPress's own\ntables**, which it reuses for content-shaped data, and **WooCommerce's own\ntables**, added for the things WordPress has no concept of — order line\nitems, tax rates, sessions, payment tokens, and fast lookup copies of hot\nfields. Knowing which tier a piece of data lives in is what makes a query\ncorrect instead of merely working today."],[893,"WooCommerce's two HTTP APIs — the wc/v3 REST API for server-to-server integration and the wc/store/v1 Store API for the browser — their auth models, how to add custom routes, and when to use which."],[270,"WooDoc also uses the standard Fumadocs components: `Callout`, `Cards` /\n`Card`, `Tabs`, `Steps`, and `Accordion`."],[116,"WooDoc currently documents against these versions. Version-specific behavior\nis called out explicitly, and differences between **classic templates** and\n**WooCommerce Blocks** are noted where they matter."],[114,"WooDoc distinguishes **recommended extension** (official hooks, filters,\nAPIs, supported template mechanisms) from **risky customization** (template\noverrides, replacing behavior) and **dangerous customization** (editing core\nfiles, relying on fragile internals). &#x2A;Just because you can change it there\ndoes not mean you should.*"],[383,"WooDoc is a **developer-first WooCommerce documentation and engineering\nreference**."],[92,"WooDoc is a developer-first WooCommerce engineering reference. This page explains what it is, who it is for, the problem it solves, and where to begin."],[117,"WooDoc is being built incrementally. This first release establishes the\nfoundation: the project definition, the information architecture, the content\ndesign system, and the first conceptual chapters. It deliberately does **not**\ntry to document every hook, template, setting, or database table yet — that is\nwhat later phases are for. See the Roadmap."],[460,"WooDoc is built incrementally. The full project is expected to take on the\norder of **10–15 working days or more**, developed in phases. This page\nsequences them. **Content quality outranks content quantity** at every phase."],[105,"WooDoc is written for **WordPress developers moving into WooCommerce**."],[206,"WooDoc scales by **composition, not prose**. Every page is assembled from a\nsmall set of page templates and content components. This page is the reference\nfor both, and it doubles as a live component gallery."],[408,"WooDoc serves all three through **progressive complexity**: plain explanation\nfirst, precise developer terminology second."],[300,"WooDoc tags pages with a priority so contributors and readers know what is\nfoundational versus specialized."],[411,"WooDoc teaches developers to think differently. Instead of only asking &#x2A;\"how\ndo I change this button?\"*, a developer learns to ask, in order:"],[1451,"WooDoc will not pretend WooCommerce is always the answer. A recurring theme in\nlater chapters is &#x2A;\"here is what this costs, here is where it strains, here is\nwhen to reach for something else.\"*"],[373,"WooDoc's mission, audience, the problem it solves, its philosophy, what makes it different, and its developer-experience principles."],[1339,"WooDoc's signature pattern for tracing any WooCommerce element back to the layer that produced it — and deciding where it is safe to change."],[1222,"WordPress Fundamentals"],[470,"WordPress fundamentals as they relate to WooCommerce (request lifecycle,\npost types, meta, options, hooks, cron, REST)."],[1277,"WordPress has \"cron\" (`wp_schedule_event()`, the `wp_cron` option) that runs\ndue tasks on regular page loads — not a real system cron unless wired to one.\nIt is fine for light, non-urgent work and unreliable for anything precise or\nhigh-volume."],[1380,"WordPress is a content management system. It manages *pages of content* and\nthe people who edit them. It has no concept of a product, a price, a cart, an\norder, a customer, tax, shipping, or a payment."],[1452,"WordPress manages content; **WooCommerce adds the entire commerce domain**\non top of it."],[533,"WordPress post meta"],[1281,"WordPress ships a REST API under `/wp-json/`. WooCommerce adds two distinct\nAPI surfaces on top:"],[1237,"WordPress stores most editable content as rows in one table (`wp_posts`),\ndistinguished by a `post_type` column. `post` and `page` are built in;\nplugins register their own with `register_post_type()`."],[515,"WordPress table"],[131,"WordPress's object cache (transient/`wp_cache_*` API) is a no-op back end by\ndefault — `wp_cache_set()` still \"works\" but nothing survives past the\nrequest unless a persistent object cache (Redis, Memcached) is installed."],[1442,"WordPress's publishing strength is a genuine advantage."],[801,"Worked before Blocks"],[1347,"Worked shape (illustrative)"],[799,"Works on the front end, not in admin/REST/cron"],[1203,"WP user with role `customer`, or a guest (session + order only)"],[461,"Write the plain explanation first, the precise terminology second."],[558,"Writing code that works in both modes"],[1160,"Writing directly to wp\\_postmeta for orders, or assuming a\nspecific table layout. Under HPOS the write goes to the wrong place or is\nsilently ignored, and reports/emails then disagree with the database."],[181,"Writing model"],[804,"Wrong priority — another callback removed or overwrote it later"],[931,"You can reuse WooCommerce's CRUD objects (`wc_get_product()`,\n`wc_get_order()`) inside your callback — do not query tables directly."],[1453,"You choose WooCommerce for the **WordPress overlap, the ecosystem, and full\ncode ownership** — and you accept **operating the stack yourself**."],[1390,"You could build this yourself as a custom plugin. WooCommerce is that plugin —\nmature, widely deployed, and with a large extension ecosystem."],[1332,"You do not need to memorize the full lifecycle now. The goal of this chapter\nis only to install the *shape* of the problem:"],[999,"You now maintain a fork of that file. Each core template has an\n@version docblock; WooCommerce → Status → System\nStatus lists every override whose version is behind core\n(\"version X is out of date. The core version is Y\"). When it flags\none, diff your copy against the new core file and re-apply your change. A\nstale override silently drops new markup, hooks, and accessibility fixes."],[1755,"You want to…"],[1323,"You will see these three labels throughout WooDoc. The recurring lesson:"],[1774,"Zones, methods, packages"]],"type":"string"},"page_id":{"docs":{"1":356,"2":357,"3":358,"4":359,"5":360,"6":361,"7":362,"8":363,"9":364,"10":365,"11":366,"12":367,"13":368,"14":369,"15":370,"16":371,"17":372,"18":373,"19":374,"20":375,"21":376,"22":377,"23":378,"24":379,"25":380,"26":381,"27":382,"28":383,"29":384,"30":385,"31":386,"32":387,"33":388,"34":389,"35":390,"36":391,"37":392,"38":393,"39":394,"40":395,"41":396,"42":397